# EcoSuit MK.I — Hemp core impregnation
**The step that turns degummed bast into a bondable SIP core**
If wet-out fails here, the ceramic fails later. This traveler starts after bake #2 / card / weave and ends at a B-staged roll or a wet-laid blank ready for cure.

---

## 0. Gate in

Do not impregnate until all of these are true:

| Gate | Number |
|---|---|
| Fiber form on hand | HEMP-W and/or HEMP-U and/or HEMP-M, CORE-LOT serialized |
| Degum complete | No slick pectin feel, rinse pH in window |
| Moisture | ≤ 2–4% by shop coupon (freeze the number) |
| Layup-room RH | Controlled; fiber out-time clock starts at bag open |
| Resin lot | PI or phenolic for flight-like cores. Toughened epoxy = ground demo only |
| Coupling agent | On or off by spec — not “some rolls yes” |
| Tool / film / solvent | Named on the traveler |

If the humidity indicator on the fiber bag has tripped, re-bake. Do not chase wet bast with extra resin.

---

## 1. What “impregnated” means

Target is not “looks wet.” Target is:

- Every bast filament wetted (no white dry islands at 10×)
- Controlled fiber volume
- Controlled resin content
- Two clean bond faces (or peel ply)
- Low enough void that bake #3 and the ceramic adhesive can finish the job
- No race-track, no resin-starved mid-plane, no crushed bast cardboard

Starting windows (coupon-tune, then freeze):

| Form | Fiber volume | Resin content (by mass, order) |
|---|---|---|
| HEMP-W skins | 45–55% Fv | ~40–55% |
| HEMP-U skins | 50–60% Fv | ~35–50% |
| HEMP-M fill | 25–40% Fv | resin-rich OK if edge-sealed later |

A resin-starved hemp core under ceramic is worse than a slightly rich one. Bast does not forgive dry centers.

---

## 2. Two legal routes (do not mix on one plate)

| | Route A — Prepreg | Route B — Wet lay / VARTM |
|---|---|---|
| Use | Flight-like cores, chest family, repeatable Fv | Large blanks, ground demo, odd shapes |
| Resin state at store | B-staged film or prepreg roll | Wet at the tool |
| Wet-out control | Calender / film weight | Vacuum fill + bleed |
| Out-time | Clock from freezer / bag | Clock from mix |
| Risk | Dry edges if film is short | Race-tracks and wet puddles |
| Preferred for EcoSuit plates | **Yes** | Only when A will not nest |

Pick one per CORE-LOT. Write it on the bag.

---

## 3. Resin systems

| System | When | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Polyimide (MAT-P / PI) | Flight-like | High Tg, outgas-capable, slow process, follow the data sheet |
| Phenolic (MAT-P) | Flight-like alternate | Good heat, water by-product in cure — bag and post-cure matter |
| Toughened epoxy (MAT-E) | Ground dummy only | Not the orbital core matrix |
| Solvent carrier | Only if the PI / phenolic route requires it | Must leave in B-stage + bake #3. No “it will flash off on orbit” |

Forbidden as the impregnation resin: random hardware-store oil, linseed “sealer,” wet polyester, anything that cannot pass TML / CVCM later.

Mix:
- Weigh resin and any hardener / catalyst to the sheet.
- Pot life on the clock.
- Degas if the sheet says so (PI and filled phenolics often do).
- No stirring air into a high-viscosity PI and calling it wet-out.

---

## 4. Optional coupling (silane class)

If the spec says coupling:

1. Apply after degum and bake #2, before impregnation.
2. Dilute per the silane sheet. Do not dump concentrate on a roll.
3. Even pickup — dip + nip or spray + dwell.
4. Dry to the sheet. This is not a third moisture bake of the bast; it is a finish.
5. Record lot, concentration, dwell, dry.

If the spec says no coupling, do not “improve” a roll at the tank. Mixed coupling inside one CORE-LOT makes the ceramic bond untestable.

